I’m implementing the native certificate now. The instance will contain explicit elliptic-curve branch points over \(\mathbb F_{p^2}\), the distinguished incoming kernel that enforces non-backtracking, and the target curve. A candidate is a fixed-length binary isogeny word. The checker will replay every quotient exactly—no floats, lookup tables, or access to the planted answer—and accept any word that reaches an isomorphic target.
codex
Step 0 points to Track B, not rejection. The paper’s own general attack is \(\widetilde O(p)\) in genus 2 (Theorem 1), while product endpoints reduce to two elliptic searches (Lemma 1 and Section 2). I’m using a native change-of-variables trapdoor: two isomorphic target presentations carry a short mixed-radix path code. That leaves a few dozen exact decoding operations after the insight, versus a measured meet-in-the-middle isogeny search.
